today's question this is a really  interesting question and I think it  comes down to the genre spaces that  exist or rather don't exist across  different media horror as a genre of  play be it stage radio or screen is a  well-established genre and well explored  in fact the very notion of a scary story  is fairly pervasive in our society the  idea of scary music on the other hand  well even just saying it out loud feels  somewhat absurd for whatever complicated  reasons song isn't a well accepted  medium for delivery of a scary story  this is in no small part why Evil Dead  the musical is ultimately a comedy and  why people find Sweeney Todd's tone to  be you know weird and even those are  examples where song is being mixed with  stage and screen mediums where horror is  already well accepted at the same time  though this isn't limited to horror if I  said oh it's an action album that sounds  like nonsense even if an album was  nothing but a tea style cinematic rock  anthems we still wouldn't describe it as  an action album at best it would seem  like a cute way of describing something  a little unconventional  at the same time we would find it weird  to describe a movie as say punk rock or  folk pop do you know is a very folk pop  movie it sounds weird but you kind to  get it even then it's really only  helpful if you've already seen Juno and  are in on the joke and can go oh yeah I  see what you're saying  cute it doesn't say anything about  content or structure only tone so to  loop back to your specific example I  would say there's an added element that  audiences don't generally conceptualize  metal as a storytelling genre of music  they wouldn't expect it to generate  something like one tin soldier or  nautical disaster despite the fairly  mainstream evidence to the contrary like  Metallica's one when you stack  moralizing on top of that it's a lot  easier to frame the music as being  purely the expression of individual  intent and not a storytelling narrative  device and this perception is not really  helped by the fact that the vocals in a  lot of metal and its various subgenres  are frankly unintelligible which lends  some weight to the idea that you know  messages are being hidden in the music  even if that idea is kind of balderdash  and nonsense  bottom line audiences don't  think about music as a way to tell a  horror story so thanks that's a great  question there's a lot more to think  about in there like why do John Roos  develop differently across different  mediums you can let me know what you  think in the comments and as always if  you've got a question you can submit it  through ask fm or Twitter now go and  peace my children in the name of like  share and subscribe  amen
 

